      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions: Spain Retirement Visa for South Africans</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>What is the Spain retirement visa for South Africans?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>The Spain retirement visa for South African citizens is the Non-Lucrative Visa (NLV). It allows South Africans to live in Spain long-term without working, provided they demonstrate sufficient passive income — approximately €2,400/month for a single applicant in 2026. South African pension annuities, living annuity drawdowns, investment income, and rental income all count. Applications are submitted in person at the Spanish Consulate in Johannesburg.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>What is the SAPS criminal record certificate for Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>South African NLV applicants must provide a SAPS (South African Police Service) Police Clearance Certificate. Apply at any SAPS police station with fingerprints. Processing typically takes 4–8 weeks. The SAPS certificate must be apostilled through DIRCO (Department of International Relations and Cooperation) and translated into Spanish by a sworn translator recognized by the Spanish consulate.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does the Rand exchange rate affect my Spain retirement visa application?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes significantly. The income threshold of €2,400/month translates to approximately ZAR 48,000–54,000/month at current rates. The Rand has historically weakened against the Euro — income that appears sufficient when planning may fall short by application time. Spanish consulates assess income in Euros at the time of application. Maintain a 15–20% buffer above the minimum threshold to account for exchange rate fluctuations.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does South Africa have a double tax treaty with Spain?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>South Africa does not have a comprehensive double taxation agreement with Spain. This creates a potential risk of income being taxed in both countries — South Africa at source and Spain as your country of residence. Unlike UK, US, Canadian, or Australian retirees who have treaty protection, South Africans face greater tax planning complexity. Professional advice from a cross-border tax specialist is strongly recommended before making the move.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>How much does it cost to apply for the Spain retirement visa from South Africa?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Total NLV application costs for South Africans typically include: SAPS clearance (~ZAR 150), DIRCO apostille (~ZAR 200 per document), sworn Spanish translations (~ZAR 800–2,000 per document), medical certificate (~ZAR 1,000–2,500), private health insurance (~ZAR 24,000–60,000/year depending on age), and the consulate visa fee (~€80–€150). Total application costs typically range from ZAR 30,000–80,000 depending on individual circumstances.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

